1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Edwin Falk Eliel, the 4th of 9 children of Louis Eliel and Minka Danziger, was born in Chicago, Illinois in 1862. His most impressive childhood memory was of the Great Chicago Fire of 1871. The fire started in a ba
2 CONC rn on October 8, burned for 2 days, killed hundreds of people and destroyed 4 square miles, which was most of Chicago at that time. The Eliels and everyone who could, fled to the shores of Lake Michigan and remained on the beach u
2 CONC ntil the fire was put out. The Eliels did not lose their home or their business, but most of the city had to be rebuilt.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Edwin was 25, he became engaged to his first cousin, Elfriede, who lived in LaSalle, Illinois. Their fathers, brothers Louis and Jeremiah Eliel, had a brewery in LaSalle and distributed their beer in Chicago. Edwin's parents,
2 CONC  Louis Eliel and Minka Danziger, were first cousins, but by 1887 when Edwin became engaged, Illinois had outlawed any further marriages between such close relatives. Edwin rented a room in Kenosha, Wisconsin, which is just over th
2 CONC e Illinois state line, and made that his legal residence. He commuted to Chicago by the 4:28 a.m. train every morning. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 11, 1888, Edwin and Elfriede were married at the home of a Congregational minister in Kenosha. Their oldest brothers were the two witnesses. Although the Eliel family was Jewish, Elfriede's mother, Alvina (and Alvina'
2 CONC s family on both sides) were Lutheran, so Elfriede had been brought up as a Christian. Elfriede and Edwin's two sons, Robert and Arthur, were brought up in a Congregational (Protestant) church in Chicago.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edwin worked as an accountant all of his life. He and Elfriede were married until her death in 1917. After that Edwin lived with his son Robert and Robert's wife Claire. Later he lived with his son Arthur while Arthur was still un
2 CONC married. Edwin died in Chicago in 1934.  Edwin and Elfriede are buried in Rose Hill Cemetery in Chicago. 
