1 NOTE {geni:about_me} I crossed the border into Switzerland in the night of 15/16 August 1938 at a point where the Alt Rhein (the original river bed) was nearly dried out, a stretch of marsh overgrown with reed and interspersed with pud
2 CONC dles and pools of water, some of them quite deep.  There was a camp at Diepoldsau, in the middle of the Rhine loop where the Swiss border guards put people that they caught in the marshes for interrogation.  We succeeded in dodgin
2 CONC g them and getting a lift under Canvas to Zurich.  The man who picked us up between Heerbrugg and Bulgach in the Rhine valley (a Mr Geiger) unloaded us in a warehouse and gave us directions to certain refugee centres.  The one we 
2 CONC went to demanded proof of identity which I promised to furnish as soon as possible.  We were sent to the Kaspar Eshu Haus (Police HQ) where I was interviewed.  I said I’m a refugee from Hitler because I fear persecution (I couldn’
2 CONC t say I’m a draft dodger because that was the surest way of being sent back – there were hundreds of “holidaymakers” in Switzerland for that reason).  The borders were already closed and strictly controlled by August 1938.  Any yo
2 CONC ung man of conscript age or a reservist from the Austrian army had to apply to the German Authorities for an exit visa (which did not mean you qualified automatically for an entry visa into Switzerland or any where else.  You had 
2 CONC to apply for that regardless and have someone vouch for you).
2 CONT
2 CONT I told you how the Wehrmacht turned me down on flimsy evidence (to my relief) in a rather apologetic manner, because I couldn’t swear an oath of allegiance to the Fuehrer, “as the law stands”.  I set up a post restante address at 
2 CONC the Zurich GPO where I spent hours almost every day writing letters and collecting mail from Vienna.  As by that time all the mail was officially opened we had agreed beforehand certain codes (eg re Wehrmacht news) to avoid troubl
2 CONC e and reprisals. By September the Military Police had already come to look for me, my mum referred them to the Wehrmacht office who gave me clearance.  They said that was only provisional, all is forgiven and I should return forth
2 CONC with and report to the barracks.  They came back twice (I think) and told my mum I’ll lose my German citizenship if I don’t return.  Not every one knew that I had fled, not even Aunty Lisi who had left home to join her boyfriend H
2 CONC ..  She wasn’t told for fear of spilling it all (she always talked too much, could never keep anything to herself.  For this reason she was kept in ignorance of family matters to prevent her carless talk unwittingly putting our da
2 CONC d at risk).
2 CONT
2 CONT My time in Zurich would now probably be rated as extreme hardship, but I wasn’t aware of it and distracted by more urgent business.  The Refugee Commission gave us 10 Swiss Francs per week, of which I paid 7 Swiss Francs for a roo
2 CONC m.  With the remaining 3 Swiss Francs I had to feed myslef (my dad sent me return coupons for stamps).  There was a Volkskueche with a daily minced loaf and a piece of bread (better than anything of this sort I ever had here), gra
2 CONC pes were plentiful and extremely cheap and so was a certain type of open chocolate and a tasty bun.  Once a week I was invited to a dinner.  There was also an old friend of the family, the ex-Vienna footballer Toegel who played fo
2 CONC r Grasshoppers.  He occasionally stood me sandwiches and drinks in a Buffet or we had a snack in his attic room.  He was still under contract and dreaded the time of return (to a dragon of a woman) and the possibility of military 
2 CONC service (he was 30 – conscription was introduced in 1936).  I don’t know what happened to him.  The papers I wanted were not forthcoming (birth certificate of my dad which would also give his parents’ details to prove the Jewish c
2 CONC onnection).  This part of our heritage was always strictly kept away from us, thanks to our mum who obviously felt ashamed of the sin she had committed by marrying outside of her religion (more of it later).
2 CONT
2 CONT My dad always described and signed himself as “konfessionslos” (no religion, freethinker) which was more or less the badge of all of those who left their church because they didn’t want to pay the church tax.  What he was before, 
2 CONC I do not know.  Adolf was a born Jew.  I don’t know if his wife was or if they were baptized like his brother W. (who had a Catholic wife and family).  I once met a Dr Worm from Halle, obviously a relative from Adolf’s in-laws, wh
2 CONC o didn’t look Jewish at all.
2 CONT
2 CONT Without the required evidence the Refugee Commission dropped me and my case was taken up by a Mr Deutsch (a clergy man without a diocese) who did social work and had contact with the Quakers (a worldwide protest organisation which
2 CONC  originated in Sweden, I think).  He got me transferred to his mate L. Salzgeber, who was also a social worker (with American experience) in charge of a youth hostel in W’hsn (I told you about it).  I had my full board and lodging
2 CONC  (rather frugal and primitive), but no money.  I was eventually transferred to his brother’s farm on Schaeflisberg, St Margereten (I told you about my duties there – cheap labour and no money – and his hostile farmworker Werner I 
2 CONC had to share an attic with – he was a bed wetter.  Salzgeber and Deutsch had undertaken to get me out of the country, after two extensions (an unregistered period for which they were responsible – maybe a higher churchman interven
2 CONC ed).  Be that as it may, I obtained a “Staatenlosenausweis” (a sort of stateless identity document) in lieu of a valid passport.  I was also served with the final order to quit the country because of “Entfremdung” (Alienisation). 
2 CONC  I was already in Zurich waiting to pick up my visa from the British consulate (for a farm in Kells, Ireland, which was still a British dominion).  I had to wait a day longer than stipulated on my deposition order and spent the la
2 CONC st night in a sort of doss house in Niederdorf (a room with 10 beds with only one other occupied).  At 6am I was rudely awoken from sleep by the Aliens Police who were going to arrest me.  After checking my papers and single ticke
2 CONC t to Dublin, they escorted me to the Hauptbahnhof and issued the final warning of jail and forceful expulsion if I didn’t leave by the stipulated train.  This ended my year’s stay in Switzerland.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Later I found out that some of my old mates has already been called up for the conquest of Czechoslovakia.  Luckily for them, as it turned out, they had marched in without a shot being fired).
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of my old classmates also left Austria before the war. K. Winkler (a Catholic with no Jewish ancestors) went to Australia. I don’t know how he managed, he had temporary exemption from the Military service as a university stude
2 CONC nt.  As such you’d still be liable for service after graduation (he had missed conscription in the Austrian army).  He settled in Sydney and married a Jewish girl.  He came here twice (in the 1960’s and 1970’s) to see me.  The sec
2 CONC ond was P. Shey, of Hungarian extraction, also a Catholic. (He was the secretary of a Catholic students club).  Nobody suspected him of being Jewish tainted (his dark looks were typically Hungarian), only I did, after I discovered
2 CONC  his people were cinema owners.  He went to the states and covered his tracks, so nobody could trace him.  My old trusted mate W. Turner told me in his last letter that he met someone from the A form at the 60th anniversary celebr
2 CONC ation who was in touch with P. Shey (who split his time between New York and Belgium, no doubt on business). [We had two forms: A form from A to L (initials) and B form from M to Z.
2 CONT
2 CONT I interrupted my journey to Kells in Paris where an old acquaintance of my Dad who owed him money was supposed to pay me back some of it.  All he did was to pay me a few days’ board and lodging where he lived and pocket money for 
2 CONC fares and snacks.  On such a pittance you could not really enjoy the nightlife of Montmartre, but it was a change for me.  I ran myself into the ground to see as much as possible and hardly went to bed.  The Louvre was already clo
2 CONC sed and there were signs everywhere of preparations for war (countless conscripts thronging the streets.  They looked extremely seedy and useless in their ill-fitting uniforms and I couldn’t help thinking what a pushover for the s
2 CONC oldiers I knew.  My misgivings proved correct a year later).  On landing at Dover the Immigration Officer asked me why I’m late and I told him I didn’t realise there was a deadline on the entry visa and I visited a friend in Paris
2 CONC .  (My English was practically non-existent).  Little did I realise that he said I can’t stay in the UK and must go straight to Dublin (which I thought was part of the UK anyway).  I spent a night in London in a Salvation Army hos
2 CONC tel (horrible) and went to Leicester next morning to visit Aunty D. who worked a Skivvy for a rich family.  It took me the best part of a day to find her in Ulverscroft, a village outside Leicester.  She was very busy and didn’t h
2 CONC ave much time for me, but seemed quite happy (she had a friend in the house, a Viennese cook).  I spent a night in a nearby guesthouse and went to Dublin next morning.  At Dun Laogharie (harbour) I was nearly arrested for exceedin
2 CONC g the stipulated entry date on my travel document, but a lady from the committee (who spoke German) intervened on my behalf.  She stood me lunch at Woolworths (I didn’t realise we were in a store) and took me to Bray, which was a 
2 CONC staging post for refugees.  After a few days (sleeping on a mattress on the floor) I was sent to Kells with a man called Dr Hirschberg, who looked a typical Jew.  He was supposed to speak English and was therefore put in charge wh
2 CONC en we boarded the Belfast train with instructions to change at Drogheda.  This, being quite near, we missed and were hauled out of the train at Goraghwood (past Dundalk), on the Ulster border.  The police were called who arrested 
2 CONC us and locked us up in a small station while they made enquiries.  We had nothing to eat and there was a small vending machine with chocolate bars in it.  I asked Dr H. to lend me a coin which he grudgingly did.  It was swallowed 
2 CONC up without return and all the banging didn’t help.  This prompted Dr H. to remark on my loutish behaviour in a serious situation (he was scared).  I told him it was his English that got us into this mess, at least I didn’t pretend
2 CONC  to have any knowledge of the language., like he did.  That set the tone for our future relationship, which turned out to be very brief.  Our predicament was resolved after phone calls with Dublin and Kells had confirmed our ident
2 CONC ity and destination.  (It eventually transpired that suspicious persons were stopped at the Ulster border on the strength of some new emergency legislation – the war was only a few days away – because of IRA activity (they support
2 CONC ed the Germans).  We were arrested as German spies!  Dr H. (from Koenigsberg) had a German passport and I had a very suspicious looking Swiss travel document.
2 CONT
2 CONT When we arrived in Kells after dusk I was starving and had a go at the food cabinet in our “kitchen”, targeting the raw bacon (never seen before) which I noticed was very tough and not half as good as our Kaiserfleisch.  Dr H. poi
2 CONC nted out it should be cooked before consumption and would I kndly leave some for him.  This “kitchen” was part of our accommodation which consisted of a partitioned top of a barn converted into a room with 2 field beds, a table, 2
2 CONC  chairs, ramshackle wardrobe and washstand with bowl, jug and bucket, al these facilities to be shared.  We were on top of horse stables, with toilet (you couldn’t call it a WC), a wooden affair over a cesspit, downstairs.  Next d
2 CONC oor to us, separated by a wooden partition, was a grain loft teeming with hundreds of rats (I was used to this sort of thing and took it in my stride, Dr H. was dead scared).  We were “guests” of Capt. Nicholson and his wife Steph
2 CONC anie (Australian), who were the land and estate owners, comprising hundreds (1000’s?) of acres of farmland, a very big mansion house, herds of cattle, pigs etc., riding stables and race horses, a staff of 30-40 farm and yard worke
2 CONC rs, housemaids, butler, kitchen staff, chauffeur etc. – one of the richest landlords in Ireland and a protestant.
2 CONT
2 CONT We had no work permit and were therefore barred from paid work.  Our status was defined as “trainee farmers” with a view to emigration to Australia where the Nicholsons also had land and relatives. Farm work started at 8am (a big 
2 CONC change to Switzerland where I had to get up at 6am – 4am at harvest time) and the land dead flat, which made it child’s play compared to Salzgeber’s “Hill” farm where you had to work like a horse (he didn’t have one – hired one, o
2 CONC nce or twice to beat the weather ie get all the hay in quick).  Whether Nicholson’s kept strictly to the letter of our training arrangement (I doubt if ever there was one) I couldn’t say, I worked 8-10 hours a day, made my own bre
2 CONC akfast with things provided (bacon, eggs, bread, butter, horrible mangold jam, tea and milk.  Lunch was originally in a small room next to the big kitchen, set aside for Dr H. and me.  Later, I alone joined the kitchen staff in th
2 CONC e big kitchen (see Upstairs/Downstairs).  The fare was disgusting cold mutton every day, followed by some rice or bread pudding and custard.  At night we were on our own again.  The kitchen maid used to bring me a big bowl of soup
2 CONC  with scraps in it.
2 CONT
2 CONT My duties were divided between those of a yard boy (milking cows, feeding animals, clearing out stables) in the morning and a harvest helper (binding, stacking and loading corn, hoeing, harrowing, sowing etc.).  The whole thing re
2 CONC quired a certain positive attitude which I had in abundance, although fully aware that it was cheap labour. But so was all farm labour in Ireland, £1.50 a week for a skilled worker (who was expected to handle horses and livestock 
2 CONC and work all hours), plus a tied stone cottage with a tiny plot to grow vegetables plus some milk.  I had all that but no money.  I got on well woth the farm workers and picked up basic English quickly (Irish vowels were pronounce
2 CONC d like German vowels, eg u=u, a=a etc, and that helped).  They bought me the occasional pint, I sold them the odd second hand garment I got from the Nicholsons (pullover, shawl, shirt and a horrible pair of jodpurs (rising breeche
2 CONC s) etc) which they snapped up eagerly for a few pence.  Nicholson also gave us 50 cigarettes each per week.  I was a non smoker and sold them to Dr H.  He always had money although there was no visible source of income.  He refuse
2 CONC d to work on the farm and got away with it, never did a stroke.  I don’t know how he spent his time because I was away in the fields.  When he accused me of bringing in a lot of dirt on my boots and overalls coming back from work 
2 CONC (he kept our room “clean”, that was all he did) I told him to shut up, it’s because idle parasites like him that we find ourselves in such a situation.  He said only a fool works for nothing whereupon I gave him a dig in the ribs 
2 CONC that made him stagger (he was about 30, my size, but a weakling and completely bald).  A solicitor by profession (I don’t think he ever had a job) he gave himself airs and superiority.  He complianed about me, not realizing that t
2 CONC he Irish do not interfere with physical arguments (why don’t you hit back?).  He had a dirty habit of using the bucket as urinal, too lazy to go outside.  When I asked him to empty it he said it’s my turn, I said there are no turn
2 CONC s and slapped his face.  This scene was seen by one of the staff from a window across the yard and caused a lot of gossip and titter, to the huge embarassment of Dr H.  I never was called to account over this incident, but Dr H. w
2 CONC as removed to Dublin and henceforth exempted from “farm work”.  I had noticed time and again this selfish and antisocial attitude of individual cosmopolitain Jews.
2 CONT
2 CONT My stay in Kells came to an end after the root crop harvest (mangolds, turnips, potatoes), a backbreaking job (stooping down all day) in wet, muddy and stormy conditions.  I was transferred to a stony and rocky farm at Crusheen, C
2 CONC ounty Clare, near the south west coast where where I spent the winter 39/40.  They didn’t have any arable land, so it was only dairy farming with a few cows.  My job was mainly as lumberjack with the old man himself (I found it an
2 CONC  interesting challenge), repairing stone walls and clearing stretches of land of bushes and dense undergrowth.  The food was appalling, the filth everywhere unbelievable, the house freezing and worst of all the fleas from a string
2 CONC  of dogs, that made life unbearable.  Again, not a penny pay and no chance to barter or sell anything.  The woman (a Mrs Hartley) critcised my English (“so common”, where did I pick it up from?), and after a spell at her brother’s
2 CONC  (Captain O’Brien) farm on an island at Foynes (in the Shannon estuary) I was transferred to Mortimer’s farm at Mullagh, County Carn.  They gave me a stone cottage to live in, with an open fire place and a hanging kettle where I d
2 CONC id my cooking.  I had endless trouble starting a fire because the wood was always dripping wet.  Sometimes I gave up, drank some milk, ate a bacon sandwich and went to bed.  There was no paper (I hadn’t seen one since Dublin), no 
2 CONC books to read ot learn from.  I had to rely for war news on what the fram workers told me.  I soon found out (in Kells) that they all hated England and their employers and hoped that Germany would win the war.  About Hitler they k
2 CONC new nothing, nor about the Jews, and couldn’t care less in their appalling ignorance, poverty and illiteracy.  Since I was more or less one of them they also assumed that I hated the landlords and the British. It didn’t occur to t
2 CONC hem why I was in Ireland at all.  If I had told them that I didn’t want to serve the Fuehrer, it would have gone down badly – running away from a fight was the last straw.  When I got a nasty cold (no wonder, the floor was just pl
2 CONC ain damp earth) a dirty old woman came twice or three times a day to look after me (I was in bed) and the fire (which tended to go out) and make some tea.  My job there was mostly with livestock and some aspects of it were really 
2 CONC disgusting, eg castrating sheep and pigs with a dirty old penknife and cutting cattle’s horns for transport to England (two men would wrestle the beast down with the aid of a rope, lock its head with a certain grip and I would cut
2 CONC  the horns off with a hacksaw (much easier than steel pipes).  With the blood spurting out of the two holes in their heads like a fountain.  It was spring 1940, I couldn’t get much information about the war, which apart from the c
2 CONC onquest of Poland the year before, hardly seemed to have started yet.  On a neighbouring farm was a young Austrian, George Liss, who became a (short lived) friend of mine.  He had some Jewish ancestors, but was younger than me and
2 CONC  below conscription age.  Like the rest of them he had travelled on a normal passport, which means he must have got clearance from the Wehrmacht to get it.  He was one of only a hadful of refugees who worked on the land like me.  
2 CONC In fact he was so enthusiastic about it, that he wanted to be a farmer after the war (he had no trade or profession, only just finished the equivalent of A Levels.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were about 200 refugees from Hitler in Ireland, probably not many more in Wales and Scotland, but several thousand in England.  Most them were interned at the beginning of the war, together with neutrals and Nazis.  Those wi
2 CONC th inflential sponsors (like Aunty D’s) escaped.  There was no internment in Ireland, not even the Nazis and draft dodgers from Germany.  The Catholics were lucky in having very rich sponsors (eg Press lords, property owners and b
2 CONC usinessmen who maintained them in idleness and paid those with higher aspirations their school and university fees.  They all lived in comfort without doing a stroke.  One of them was S. Feric (“Sir Hubert”) my old mate from the S
2 CONC ocialist Students’ Gym Club in Vienna.  His sponsor was a Mrs Lentaigne of Dublin who owned (among other) property in Westbourne Terrace.  He had to abandon his studies at Vienna University and she paid him all the fees and mainte
2 CONC nance to continue at UCD (Medical studies).  I’m sure had more than you ever had with all your grants, yet he dropped aout after a year and pursued a life of idleness until the end of the war, paid for by Mrs L.
2 CONT
2 CONT The protestants were relatively few and their sponsors were rich landowners like Nicholsons and Mortimers.  As I told you they were tight fisted and mean and wanted vlaue for giving board and lodging.  The Jews (by religion) lived
2 CONC  in Dublin and seemed to be doing well, without officially engaging in work.  (I believe the local Jews involved tham in their businesses, never mind a work permit).
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1940 I had a spell at Maxwell’s farm in Kilcormae.  Mr M. was absent in England, his wife rarely seen on the farm which was small and run by a steward.  There was nothing memorable about it except that we had bacon and cabb
2 CONC age every day.  One day a car drew up outside the house where I had a bedroom on the 1st floor.  I was told to pack up my things in a hurry, I’ll be taken somewhere else.  With nothing to lose it seemed to be a welcome chance to b
2 CONC reak the old monotony and demonstrating my eagerness, I even threw my rucksack out of the window.  We didn’t travel far, just over the Tipperary border to a place called Lorrha, where my new slave owners, the Kennys (protestants, 
2 CONC of course) had a big farm.  It seemd that they had done an underhand deal with the Maxwells who were friends and this was bound to have repercussions.  My job consisted of yard work and harvest work and I had found a friend in the
2 CONC  daughter of the house, a spinster more than twice my age who undertook to teach me proper English.  She was more intelligent than the others, and there was a local paper to read, she had a radio and we could discuss the news (Fra
2 CONC nce had just been beaten and surrendered to Germany).  I had a decent room to myself, more comfort than anywhere before and a free Sunday with breakfast in bed!  We went boating, swimming and fishing in the Shannon and we visited 
2 CONC friends on other farms.  In July we had a 2 weeks’ caravan holiday in Galway Bay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Each refugee was issed with an Aliens police book in which every move was entered by the aliens police (change of address, journeys, jobs – even the shortest, temporary and unpaid ones, had to be reported without delay to the alie
2 CONC ns police).  This condition was lifted in our case in 1960, 21 years after arrival in these islands. [This might help you to understand my attitude towards the British as a whole (allowing for the fact that they didn’t as me to co
2 CONC me here, but rather the other way round, a fact that some grumbling Jews, eg Sir Hubert, conveniently forgot) and to the modern demanding type of refugee in particular]
2 CONT
2 CONT To come back to Lorrha and the Kennys, one day a summons arrived for me to appear in court at Birr, County Offally, on a charge of contravening the aliens act.  Although my English was by then good enough for everyday use, I didn’
2 CONC t understand the letter, but the Kennys seemed to be concerned.  Still, I felt completely innocent and couldn’t care less.  The hearing was scheduled on the following day, the “family solicitor” turned out for the prosecution, the
2 CONC re was panic when, at the last moment, a lady from the joint refugee commission in Dublin appeared on the scene as my witness and counsel.  I had left Lorrha on my own on a bike for the 10 mile ride (my mate Chris and I used to cy
2 CONC cle to Sunday cinema in Birr, so I knew the way) and took a leisurely hour, which left only a few minutes briefing before we entered court.  The hearing seemed a monotonous repitition of the gravity of my offence in spite of the f
2 CONC act that I made an unreserved confession of my crime, as instructed, and kept saying “I know, I should have done, but I didn’t…”.  The lady from Dublin vouched for my good character, including my god-fearing upbringing and devotio
2 CONC n to the church that helped me in my present predicament as a refugee from Nazi persecution. The whole proceedings struck me as rum do, the only thing that annoyed me was that I was referred to as a “Christian Jew”.  I got away wi
2 CONC th a caution.  Still, there were only a handful of people in court, surely the whole episode would soon be forgotten?  When Hilda Kenny, my tutor and mentor, produced the next issue of the local paper and pointed to a lengthy repo
2 CONC rt of my court case, I was mortified, inspite of the fact that it was safe to assume that some of the people I actually worked with couldn’t read and write.  There it was for everyone to see: Mr Morgernstern, a Christian Jew, comm
2 CONC itted the serious offence of changing address without reporting to the police, considering the dangerous times we live in etc…  The people I worked with on the farm didn’t know my name, nor why I was there at all, they were friend
2 CONC ly (very important, considering the hatred against all foreigners in those far off days, 55 years ago), helpful and didn’t ask questions.  And now this bombshell, which was the Kenny’s fault (all other “employers” had strictly com
2 CONC plied with the rules and reported my moves to the alien police), they had forgotten or ignored the basic rule after “kidnapping” me from the Maxwell’s farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was anxious to get away from the place and out of farming altogether which seemed such a waste of time after the pretence of “Training for Australia” had finally been dropped.  Taking stock of myself at this juncture was very de
2 CONC pressing.  At nearly 24 I never had a normal paid taxable job of any description, let alone in my profession, since I left the TGM at 18 and a half with a national certificate in electrical engineering.  The three years (35-38) in
2 CONC  Vienna were, of necessity, devoted to “Dienst” (Service) ie Arbietsdienst (voluntary labour scheme for unemployed young people, later made compulsory (1 year service) under Hitler) and Pflichdienst (as an army conscript).  These 
2 CONC were useful qualifications for someone lined up for a job or promotion but in my case only stop gap activities.  The biggest stumbling block in Austria was my name – not an excuse, the proof was in the pudding.  As one of the top 
2 CONC 5 in our class I had to watch as everybody got a job, including the bottom group of dunces with countless exam retakes between them (that was long before Hitler).  My exit from Austria was (then) therefore not a step back but, at 
2 CONC worst, a step sideways (or so I thought).  It must be said here that if we had been Jews we would, like most of them, have fared a lot better.  They were a clan and law unto themselves whose motif in life was money and enrichment 
2 CONC regardless at whose expense.  As an unmolested Austrian citizen with equal rights and opportunities I wouldn’t have run away but mucked in with the rest and hoped for the best.  (All but 2 of my mates survived the war ie 20 out of
2 CONC  22).  I loved my beautiful country too much to abandon it carelessly.  Back on track: Apart from G. Liss there was no one else left working on a farm so I had to think of a ruse to get out of it.  (Originally I was prepared to se
2 CONC e it through just to get to Australia in the hope of finding better work and more congenial conditions there).  I was now able to write a letter of some sort (maybe not quite to Aunty D.’s present standard) and decided to get a mo
2 CONC ve on.  I had developed a varicose vein on my right leg and pretended to be in pain.  I wrote to Mrs H. (the lady that came to my rescue, she represented the protestants on the refugee committee) and to Sir Hubert to arrange a con
2 CONC sultation (in the outpatients’) with his doctor friend  at St Vincent hospital, Dublin.  It resulted in a minor operation, a ligation under local anaesthetic (20 minutes) as a guinea pig in front of students.  The important thing 
2 CONC was the post-op rehabilitation period , arranged with the doctor’s and Sir Hubert’s connivance (normally you step off the table and start walking as much as possible).  I was granted 6 months respite from farmwork on doctor’s orde
2 CONC rs, moved into a Dublin boarding house and enrolled in evening classes at a nearby technical college (City and Guilds course in electrical engineering).  My F/B was paid by the committee (27/6 per week, approximately £1.355), but 
2 CONC I got no money.  However, I derived some pathetic revenue from the discarded clothes given to me by various well wishers which I took straight to the pawn shop.  It always amazed me the rubbish they took (so much for the living st
2 CONC andards in Ireland at the time. I personally lived for many years in handed down clothes).  What’s more, I usually flogged the pawn ticket just as quick for a few pence.  When Mrs H. asked me why I didn’t wear the nice suit she ga
2 CONC ve me, I said I’m having it altered.  No question how I manage this without income (for fear of having to fork out some herself).  In class nearly all my mates were working for the Dublin electricity board and did the course for p
2 CONC romotion.  One of them (from a public school) who was a manager in a Drogheda factory was such a dunce that I had to give him paid lessons.  He used to take me for fish and chips and a drink after class (I had enough retained from
2 CONC  TGM to sail through this class without effort).  There were other casual jobs (eg digging up a garden) so that I usually had enough money for a pint (5p) or a dance (10p), matinee cinema (2p) etc., but hardly ever had a 10/- (50p
2 CONC ) let alone a £1 note in my pocket.  Before I came to Dublin I had never seen any of these 2 notes.  I changed residence several times and found I was better off with just a room (with a “gas ring”) and self catering (mostly liver
2 CONC , almost thrown at you in the market, duck eggs (green, but larger and half price) and bread.  Fish and chips very cheap at 3p for a huge portion.  On Sunday a good roast beef dinner at 1/6 (8p).  In the autumn of 1941 all this ca
2 CONC me to a temporary end when I was sent back to Kells to help out with the root crops.  When I came back to Dublin and called on my old landlady to collect my best suit I had left for safekeeping in her wardrobe, she burst into tear
2 CONC s and produced a pawn ticket which she was unable to redeem.   I told her to keep it and maybe she could pay me back in instalments.  (She never did).
2 CONT
2 CONT Before the end of 1941 the work permit ban was relaxed in individual cases (when native Irish couldn’t be found to fill certain jobs).  There was little skill in Ireland, it was a land of peasants.  Most of the unkilled labour had
2 CONC  gone to England to do “war work” in factories, bomb damage repair, transport etc.  Dublin port had become very busy and its Liffey dockyard was repairing war damaged British cargo ships and degaussing others against magnetic mine
2 CONC s.  I was referred to a Mr Gerry Owens who was secretary of the ETU Dublin branch.  He steamrollered my application for membership through against the fierce opposition of at least 90% of his membership (easy to estimate by the nu
2 CONC mber of raised hands at each vote).  In the face of all this hostility of my new “brothers” I started the job with great trepidation and misgivings, expecting the sack in the first week.  I didn’t have a clue about ship wiring, ha
2 CONC d never done screwed barrel, lead work (long since condemned), steel trunking etc.  I could never hope to ask anyone for information (eg British tube and wire gauges, screw clip, saddle and box sizes etc.) let alone help (with blo
2 CONC ck, steel tape, cutters etc.) – a real nightmare and I was going to be paid the same rates as the old expeienced hands who all hated me from the start because of the way their legitimate votes and fears were overruled.  My only fa
2 CONC int hope was to surreptitiously pick up from what they were doing and plead for time to adopt the new ways.  I asked G.O. if he couldn’t start me as a mate, I don’t mind less pay but he said it’s impossible, he had already told th
2 CONC e bloke in charge, O’Connel, that I’m fully qualified and anyway, why should I worry, I had a C&G qualification, which none of the others had.  I soon found out that the work was divided and subdivided to an incredible extent, tha
2 CONC t there was little individual skill, a lot of time wasting due to demarcation disputes and over manning, and given a few days to tide me over, I’ll be able to hold my own.  G.O. had given me a tool box with a few battered tools, n
2 CONC ot enough to do a real job with.  I was afraid that one or the other would remark on this but in the end I found out that secret was to fall in with the Brothers, never mind the quality of work and they’ll accept you and even lend
2 CONC  you tools.  You were not allowed to do this and that, eg drill a hole for a cable fixing.  Your job was to measure it out, centre punch it and wait for “Mick the Driller” whose only job was to drill holes that had been marked.  D
2 CONC egaussing was more or less a labourer’s job, pulling cables round the ship’s hull through holes cut by the “Burner”.  The hardship of the job was appalling, you had to endure the icy, unbroken storms on open decks all day, sometim
2 CONC es till late in the night when on ovetime.  To get to the dockyard on your bike early morning in almost pitch darkness (a few yellow lights) along the quayside was sheer torture.  You often had to dismount so as not to be blown of
2 CONC f your bike and the harbour was criss-crossed by rails from carnes and sidings.  The money was excellent, £4 17/6d per week, (I should think equivalent to £350 now), and there was a subsidised canteen with eggs and chips everyday.
2 CONT
2 CONT I worked on this throughout the winter of 41/42, with one recall in the spring.  I had missed the C&G final course and found myself unemployed once more.  I realised this was no life, the opportunities lay in England, that’s where
2 CONC  all the enterprising people went.  The only question was how to surmount the obstacles placed in our way, to get there.  In the meantime a Pioneer Corps for foreigners was to be set up and attached to the British army overseas.  
2 CONC It took a long time to come into being and the conditions of joining and criteria of acceptance were never explained.  My ex-flat mate Karl Steiner had graduated as a doctor (medical) at UCD (Dublin?), volunteered for service with
2 CONC  the British army and was snapped up right away and given a commission as army doctor.  What ineterested me most was that he changed his name to Sheridan (on the advice of the army).  He was a nice chap, always studying – no enter
2 CONC tainment.  His dad was a colonel (retired) in the Austrian army, his mum was a jew.  He never said what happened to them.  Anyway, it struck me, here was a solution to my biggest problem, the only chance to change my name (otherwi
2 CONC se impossible at the time for the likes of us).  It was enough motivation to volunteer for the new Pioneer Corps.  There were six of us that went to Belfast recruiting centre for medical and interview (a sort of political vetting)
2 CONC .  Of course we all said we want to fight Hitler for what he’s done to us.  We were told we’ll be woking behind the lines to enable British troops to take up more strategic positions.  We were all sent back to Dublin and told to w
2 CONC ait for the call up.  This took some time and in the end only three were accpeted (I wasn’t).
2 CONT
2 CONT My immediate concern was not to be sent back to the land.  Funds were low (of the refugee organisation), the vast majority of the refugees chose to stay in safe Ireland and typically, the most vociferous Jews who now had a chance 
2 CONC to fight Hitler failed to volunteer, among them Sir Hubert.  He had more chances than most, limitless time for carefree study to achieve a medical degree and follow in Steiner’s footsteps (he didn’t like to be reminded of that).  
2 CONC The refugee commission had tried since 1941 to get me and a few others work pemits for England.  The difficulty was that Ireland exercised its right as a British Dominion to stay neutral and harboured a number of Germans, some of 
2 CONC whom paraded as refugees.  In other words it was hardly worth the British’s trouble to screen out those few reliably loyal to the British cause, even if they had some useful skills.  The only exceptions were doctors and other medi
2 CONC cal staff.  The main concern of the British was to contain Ireland ie render it hamless for the duration of the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT The jewish refugees in Dublin seemed to be doing well and all had money and some already businesses and houses by the end of 1942.  I had enrolled for the final C&G course in the naïve hope (as it turned out) that the final certif
2 CONC icate would be a passport to all sorts of jobs in England (supported in this belief by my classmates for whom it was the peak of achievement – no need to aim higher for the best jobs on the board).  Just to make sure, I took a cou
2 CONC rse in spot welding.  At the beginning of 1943 I was offered a job in a Dublin zip factory, run by a jewish refugee from Vienna, who also had a hat factory in Galway.  The manager (Blaskopf) and his mate (Lowy) were also jewish re
2 CONC fugees (with plenty of money).  The lynchpin of the business was a Mr Oberer, a shamelessly self confessed Nazi whose motives for leaving Vienna to follow his jewish boss were not quite clear.  He was an excellent toolmaker, preci
2 CONC sion mechanic and designer who could command any salary in Germany or U.K.  I was assigned to him as a mechanic and technical assistant at £2.50 per week (the average unskilled wage was £3).  He took to me like a brother and I bec
2 CONC ame indispensable right away because he couldn’t speak English.  That seemed to be the stranglehold the Jews had over him, although without him the factory couldn’t last a day.  He treated his jewish bosses like dirt, shouted obsc
2 CONC enities at them, locked his workshop and let L&B hammer at the door. I even saw him rough handling his boss Lowy and throwing him out.  Nobody dared to touch him for fear of losing him and with him the works.  Oberer had an inferi
2 CONC oty complex about his English and that could have been the trump card fo the jews, a sort of compensation for their perpetual humiliation.  I found the attitude of the jews, based on money and greed, more disgusting than Oberer’s 
2 CONC (serving the jews he despised).  Apart from this rum set up of nazi/jew in a neutral country, at eachother’s thoat but depending on eachother, the factory was a typical jewish sweatshop, with the girls receiving appalling wages.  
2 CONC Oberer got me a wage increase of 50p per week – not enough to make a splash.  I was 26, my living standard was really appalling (I never let it worry me) and I was going nowhere (which did worry me).  I finished my C&G course but 
2 CONC had to wait for results (2nd class pass) [It is the qualification that the Imperial Institute give as a sort of dressing on top your degree.  The C&G college, a huge red brick building, used to be next to the Imperial College, hen
2 CONC ce presumably the close association].  Shortly afterwards a work permit for London arrived with a job offer as a welder in a Plaistow factory (East End, by the river, the most bombed district in London).  I lost no time, walked ou
2 CONC t of the zip factory and my digs and arrived at Euston in pitch darkness, with a suitcase (which I left in the station cloakroom).  I had no accommodation and nowhere to sleep.  I took a doormat from the Euston Hotel and bedded my
2 CONC self down in a doorway, when the air raid siren went. I wasn’t used to it and got scared.  I couldn’t sleep a wink, not even after the “all clear”.  I decided to stay put, there was no point in getting panicky in the dark.  All of
2 CONC  a sudden a powerful torch was shining in my face and almost blinding me.  It was a policeman who asked me did I come from Ireland and what am I doing here (he seemed used to this sort of Irish influx).  I said yes, just arrived f
2 CONC rom Dublin and lost my way.  He didn’t ask any more questions and opened a nearby air raid shelter for me, explaining they have to be kept locked because people used them as lavatories.  I gradually sensed what I was going to be u
2 CONC p against when I reported to Bloomsbury next morning, a sort of social woker for aliens.  A woman gave me 10 shillings and directions for my journey to Plaistow.  When I arrived there was nothing to see except bomb flattened acres
2 CONC .  Nobody could help me find the factory and no had anybody ever heard of it.  When I reported back to Bloomsbury they checked details and said it’s all a mistake by the labour exchange, it must have been an old job ticket which t
2 CONC hey forgot to verify and cancel.  Since my work permit was linked only to this particular job, I’ll have to go back to Dublin because I’m not permitted to wait for a job that suits me (which would never come my way anyway) but wou
2 CONC ld have to take anything on offer.  She looked up a register and said: “here is a job as a kitchen hand at the Pastoria, Piccadiliy, split duty (morning and evening session) at £4 per week.  So here I was, with newly acquired tech
2 CONC nical qualifications and the labour market crying out for skilled people (because of a perpetual shortage due to war), being offered the dregs not even an illiterate would voluntarily accept.  (Again an incident that stuck forever
2 CONC  in my memory and maybe, helps explain why I’d rather see Germany than England win the World Cup!  The job was extremely hot and disgusting (the kitchen teeming with vermin.  I found this a common feature of nearly all catering es
2 CONC tablishments, laundries and baths.  The insects thrive on hot, damp and greasy conditions).  I had a few hours off every afternoon and went sightseeing around Westminster and West End before returning to late duty.  My first lodgi
2 CONC ngs were a hostel in Westbourne Terrace where I slept in a room with 6-8 others.  There were no facilities, just a bed and not even a hook for your coat.  The place was open all day, people (mostly Poles and other foreigners) came
2 CONC  and went, there were new faces in your room every day.  I left my medium sized suitcase (I doubt Oxfam would accept the contents today) at Euston Station and went there if I needed a shirt etc.  Garments were on coupons and you c
2 CONC ould not afford to even lose your second hand stuff.  I don’t remember how long this awful situation went on, maybe 2-3 weeks.  You had no options and depended entirely on the dregs dished out by the labour exchange to people like
2 CONC  me.  I soon discovered this applied to all refugees, they got the lowest grade jobs, whereas unskilled Irish labour got much better factory paid jobs (the Irish were not politically suspect like us, although everybody knew how th
2 CONC ey hated the British).  As a foreigner you could be still be lucky money-wise as I discovered at the Pastoria, where the head porter (in Admiral’s uniform), a Viennese Jew, collected very generous tips from US officers who went in
2 CONC  and out of the hotel all day.  He could easily make £5 a day, tremendous money at the time. It made me, stuck in that hot stinking kitchen, real jealous.  I could have gone back to Dublin (what for?) but would have died of shame 
2 CONC because I considered all those I left behind as cowards and said so.  I also had a moral obligation towards Auntie, who had moved to Thames Ditton (near Walton) 2 years (I think) earlier.  A vicar in Ennis had befriended me  and h
2 CONC is wife said her sister in Thames Ditton could do with help, the house is much smaller and Auntie would have an easier time, the hours would also be better.  The transfer was approved by the authorities (the amazing thing was that
2 CONC  they didn’t even call her up to do something more useful for the war effort instead of helping people who would normally not get domestic staff even in peace time).  The first and foremost problem was to find a job and a home for
2 CONC  Auntie and me.  There were vacant rooms for obvious reasons.  Some landladies added to the vacancy notice: “no foreigners” or “no Irish” (quite legal and taken for granted in those days, over 50 years ago).  Eventually I found an
2 CONC  attic room (like yours upstairs) at 81 Hereford Road at 10/- (=50 pence) per week.  The landlady (a German) also gave me first refusal of the next vacant room (for Auntie).  I was also assigned a better job at the Regent Street P
2 CONC oly as a maintenance engineer!  The greater part of the establishment had been converted into a training centre for pettiofficers (machine tools).  The duties were many and spread over 24 hour shifts i.e. you came on duty at 8am a
2 CONC nd signed off next day at 8am – every other day, including every other Sunday, 72 hours a week for £7 per week.  The hourly rate was marginally better than the kitchen job (ignoring “unsocial hours” of which nobody thought – after
2 CONC  all, there was a war on).  The 72 hours included ARP duties (fire watch during air raids) a moral duty accepted by factory workers and others all over the country.  When the siren went, those on night duty had to take the lift up
2 CONC  to the flat roof, spread out and watch out for incendiary bombs and douse them with stirrup pumps before they burnt holes through the roof and set it alight.  There was nothing you could do about explosions, inside or outside a b
2 CONC uilding.  If “your name was on a bomb”, so to speak, that was that.  My additional responsibilities included lift maintenance, that was vital, and secondary plant for emergency lights.  When the siren went I first of all had to ma
2 CONC ke a dash for the Poly Annexe in Little Titchfield Street (behind All Saints Church) if there was a function on (usually a dance) to check emergency lighting.  It is true to say that during my time at the Poly not a single incendi
2 CONC ary bomb fell on us although there were countless alarms and considerable destruction in the West End, Holborn and City.  The biggest damage was done 1-2 years earlier in the “Blitz”.  The Poly had a blast damaged wall which had t
2 CONC o be shored up, the Langham Hotel, its neighbour, was half destroyed.  The BBC building opposite suffered only minor damage, All Saints major damage.  On the nearby Primrose Hill (top of Regents Park) were the AA batteries and the
2 CONC  fragments of its shells that fell into the streets were actually a bigger danger than the bombs.  There were always drunken GI’s coming up Regent Street from Piccadily (nearby Conduit Street was prostitutes quarter) and we had in
2 CONC structions to drag them in from the street when the sirens went.  With the advent of V2 things took a more sinister turn.  There was no alarm and no AA defence, they just fell out of the sky.  The odds of being hit were probably s
2 CONC till great, but their impact shook a wide area and scared more than it killed.  On alarm-free nights we took turns of naps on benches in the hall.  Our Electrical shop was in the basement which was teeming with rats who fed on scr
2 CONC aps from the kitchen and canteen above.  Behind our work bench was a storage space partitioned off by hard board.  The boxes there (with old fittings, cables etc.) were full of rats, dead and alive.  I could tell by the bluebottle
2 CONC s swarming around when I came in in the morning, that my mate “Mac” had put rat poison behind the partition.  The stench was often appalling and “Mac” tried to neutralise it with carbolic.  We normally had to stay in this shop and
2 CONC  wait for calls on the intercom (if the rats hadn’t bitten the wires – we had to trace the breaks).  On the whole, we lived in comparative peace because the war seemed to have bypassed the West End in its later stages.  I got on w
2 CONC ell with everybody except the contrary Mac, but he had to give in because it would have been impossible to find him another qualified mate.  My predecessor had lost a leg when a bomb hit a pub in Mortimer Street where he used to s
2 CONC top for a pint on his way to the Annexe.  We had a swimming pool and showers which came in handy in view of the lack of facilities at home (Gas ring, Jug, Bowl and Bucket).  The canteen also helped (if you could forget the cockroa
2 CONC ches which I killed by the hundreds when servicing kitchen equipment).  On the whole, you had to remind yourself that there was a war on and that you were no worse off than millions of other people.  I could well manage on rations
2 CONC , while most people complained.  In fact, I ate better and more that I did anywhere in Ireland.  Auntie had to apply for war work before she could leave Thames Ditton.  This was easy enough to obtain but she had to wait till a roo
2 CONC m on the top floor of 81 Hereford Road, next to mine, became vacant.  She got a job as a waitress in Government Offices on top of Regents Park at an appalling wage, but of course, she could eat there.  So after 5 years separation 
2 CONC we were united again, although we didn’t see much of each other.  Rations never affected me, I didn’t even use them all.  The same applied to my clothing coupons, it all reflected the way we were brought up and the deprivations I 
2 CONC had to put up with in earlier years.  (I haven’t really changed much to this day).  Money was not a problem, there wasn’t much to spend it on except entertainment.  Covent Garden Opera was turned into a dance hall for the duration
2 CONC  of the war, always crowded and myself often there or in the nearby Astoria, Charing Cross Road.  The nearest was the Irish Club (top of Queensway) where I met some of my Dublin acquaintances.  There were others, all of them crowd
2 CONC ed and the trouble was that you had to often walk home at night in the black out.  While I felt safest in crowds (when the siren went, the band stopped and the band leader told people where to go to the nearest shelter.  Hardly an
2 CONC yone ever left and the band carried on) Aunties was more scared of bombs and spent countless nights on tube station platforms which were crowded with hundreds of people.  Towards the end of the war we were assigned places in a bas
2 CONC ement of a big block of flats near Marble Arch.  Money wise I considered myself rich.  I had saved about £120, in spite of the fact that I paid Auntie’s rent (more than mine) in view of her pathetic wages.  As the war approached i
2 CONC ts end I could feel the under current of hatred against foreigners getting stronger and I sensed a bleak future for us here.  At work I was told the regular staff electrician is coming back from the war and will be reclaiming his 
2 CONC rightful place.  I didn’t fancy making this a life job anyway, and had been brushing up on my Maths and Electrical Engineering in quiet duty spells to keep my City and Guilds alive (I needn’t have bothered).  I also attended a Fre
2 CONC nch course at the Poly language institute in Great Portland Street.  Little did I realise that apart from the “foreigner” label and name I’d never get on to a professional job register because everything else was against me. I was
2 CONC  past 28 and in the very best of cases I could (theoretically) hope to start at the level of a 19 year old HNC holder.  This would have required some patronage (where from, in this anti-alien climate and my name to boot).  I was y
2 CONC et to discover that the higher City and Guilds certificate carried no weight by itself (although it exempted me from parts of the IEE exam which was also worthless unless accompanied by an appropriate job).  A vicious circle indee
2 CONC d.  However, there were other distractions right now, we had survived the war, you could go to bed again without fear of being killed by bombs, there were bound to be millions of people who had missed out like myself, for various 
2 CONC other reasons and after all, I never intended to settle in the UK anyway.  If it hadn’t been for the war I could well have been in the US or Australia by now.  But something else that had been dormant all these years took preceden
2 CONC ce over all other considerations – what happened to our family? I was to discover before the year was out.
2 CONT
2 CONT On VE day we joined the huge crowds thronging the streets and heading for Buckingham Palace where we saw the royal family and Churchill on the balcony.  On the way down I noticed a lot of bonfires and wanton destruction (the follo
2 CONC wing winter there was a fuel shortage and people were freezing) which did not agree with my Spartan upbringing, victory or not.  In the midst of all this merry making I gradually felt like a lonely stranger who could not identify 
2 CONC with the celebrating mob.  Assailed by evil forebodings of nasty things in store for us I felt tired and disillusioned on arriving home in the early hours.  A proper killjoy, you might think, under the circumstances, when everybod
2 CONC y was celebrating the delivery from evil.  Time and again in later life I found I couldn’t join in the hysterics of crowds whose cause I approved and aims I shared.
2 CONT
2 CONT Still, life went on after VE day, but my days at the Poly were numbered.  Everybody took liberties, Mac was later than ever (the shift system was kept for the time being although the blackout was relaxed and you could bed yourself
2 CONC  down on rags, somewhere out of sight, for a night’s sleep).  We could  sit through a session (1 hour) of News Cinema, take a long lunch hour, including swimming in the pool, join the dance at the Annexe while on duty etc.  In the
2 CONC  entrance hall was the big counter of the Poly travel agency (years later to be merged with Lunn Poly) whose manager, a Major Brough (retired) had befriended me  and held out prospects of a job when things returned to normal.  The
2 CONC  Poly used to run a club with holiday chalets in Tirol and Major Brough said I’d be the very type they’ll be looking for as a host manager of such a holiday village.  Common sense told me that this sort of thing would take a few y
2 CONC ears to materialise – there were a few scores to be settled before Austria became acceptable again and I had to get a passport and citizenship of some sort since nobody would entertain a stateless person.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sooner than I expected I could tell Mac (my mate) and Mr McKenna (my boss) that I’m going back to Austria (which wasn’t quite true) because I felt duty bound to step down so that the man who fought for his country could return to 
2 CONC his job without fuss.  They tried to persuade me to stay, the Poly would expand and there’ll be jobs for everybody.  What happened was I got a letter from the labour exchange that the American CC were recruiting German speaking pe
2 CONC ople to help their army of occupation in Germany.  I could hardly believe my luck, here seemed to be an instant solution to all my current misgivings and problems.  I was there in a flash, passed a medical and German (strict) and 
2 CONC English (lenient) exam and answered a few technical questions (my electrical engineering background had been noted and registered as an additional qualification).  Within a few days I was fitted with an American uniform and all th
2 CONC e paraphernalia that went with it and found myself ina group of recruits like me on our way to Paris.  It occurred to me right away that the Yanks, who didn’t know us, took this obvious practical step without hesitation whereas th
2 CONC e British, on whose alien register we’d been for a number of years, chose to keep us in our place as aliens who couldn’t be trusted.  [Given their linguistic talent or lack of it, the British had to take on local German people of 
2 CONC dubious loyalty on trust to do the work (in their zone of occupation in northern Germany) we were doing it for the Yanks in southern Germany].  We were civilians in uniform, assigned to the CCD (civil censorship division) of the U
2 CONC S army under the command of specialist officers, all of whom were either first or second generation German-Americans.  The overall boss of the CCD was a Colonel Weissinger, two of his liaison officers I had to deal with were Capta
2 CONC in Schulz and Captain Freitag, my immediate boss was Lieutenant Theo Faust who still had two maiden aunts in Karlsruhe (he took me along to have tea with them).  All the CCD personnel had to be able to speak and write proper Germa
2 CONC n.  The likes of us reservist had to be perfect in German, familiar with all the idioms and colloquial language and able to adopt to regional dialects (Bavarian, Swabian, Baden/Pfalz, Hesse etc.) because of the nature of our job (
2 CONC telephone monitoring, listening into conversations on the phone, intercepting telegrams and mail etc.).  The object of the exercise was to catch known Nazis, suspects and others who wittingly or unwittingly helped Nazis on the run
2 CONC .  The idea was to encourage conversation and correspondence without blotting out suspicious passages, thereby gathering as much information as possible.  We had blacklists of war criminals and PGs who went into hiding, some with 
2 CONC aliases, and were expanding on this theme with ever growing lists of suspects.  Initially we were taken to Poissy, just outside Paris, for an introductory course of our duties.  In my case this included practical instructions by a
2 CONC  technical sergeant of the US army in tapping telephone lines and connecting and maintaining recording equipment (no electronics in those days!).  Recording of speech was done on gramophone disc principle by actually cutting groov
2 CONC es on a 35mm transparent tape.  We used to go to Paris after duty hours, but I didn’t recognise the city I first got to know 6 years earlier (yet there was no visible destruction!).  We saw plenty of war-damage on our way to Offen
2 CONC bach, our next stop.  We had comfortable lodgings in requisitioned private houses whose owners (usually prominent rich Nazis) had fled.  Our place of work was in nearby Frankfurt, which was half destroyed.  My next destination was
2 CONC  Esslingen (for lodgings) and Stuttgart for work, with occasional trips to Ulm and Pforzheim (to check and change connections).  The destruction everywhere was incredible, the nearest comparison was Coventry (where I spent a week’
2 CONC s holiday in 1944 as, presumably, the safest place in England because it was nearly flattened and there wasn’t anything left standing worth another bomb) which wasn’t as bad as some German cities – worst of all Pforzheim which was
2 CONC  flattened by artillery in the course of the US advance.  Through the mountains of rubble you could smell decomposing corpses that must haven been there for 6 months (the devastation in London was not so concentrated because of it
2 CONC s size, acres of destruction in the City and East End were still surrounded by houses left standing and therefore the total impression was less grim than in Germany).  Karlsruhe was my last stop and I could at last settle down to 
2 CONC my various duties at the GPO.  Out outfit consisted of approximately 20 staff (comprising monitors, supervisors, secretaries and technicians under the command of Lt. Faust)., men and women of equal grade receiving equal pay (but t
2 CONC he Yanks getting more than us).  Those from the UK were all refugees, some were Jews, others “tainted”.  We also had Danes who spoke excellent English but were not so adaptable in German.  One Dutchman who could speak but not writ
2 CONC e the language was taken off the lines and used as a driver and mechanic in the motor pool.  We from the UK had to sign a declaration waiving any claim to US citizenship on the strength of our voluntary service (there was nothing 
2 CONC to stop us applying through normal channels and waiting in the queue for the personal quota number to come up).  We were also barred from supervisory jobs for which you had to be a US citizen (Everybody had to swear an oath on the
2 CONC  US flag).  All this seemed pure logic to us from the UK and didn’t matter.  Our lives had been transformed beyond recognition, from despised aliens and relegated serfs to respected people doing an important, interesting and excit
2 CONC ing job, which carried all sorts of perks, including free access to the officers’ club. The pay of £8 per week (in US dollars) was outstanding and we could transfer some of it to England.  On top of that we had full board and lodg
2 CONC ing.  We could use coupons in the best restaurants (e.g. Karpfen in Karlsruhe and others in Heidelberg), there was duty free, including cheap stuff in the PX stores, free travel inside the US zone and of course frequent dances at 
2 CONC “Villa8” (officers’ club) with champagne by the gallon.  In the course of 1946 restrictions on German nationals were gradually relaxed and your mum was allowed to take part in this entertainment.  I had made her acquaintance befor
2 CONC e “fraternization” was officially permitted and all we could do was go for walks along the Rhine and Alb and visit her people (which nearly put me off the whole thing, but I realised she was the proverbial “edelweiss on a dung hea
2 CONC p” who emphasised the chasm that separated me from the British and Jewish mentality – a girl I could identify with).
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1946 I was due for my first leave which I spent in London to see Auntie.  I had a room in a Bayswater hotel and everything was terrible and shocking.  It made the prospect of eventually returning permanently almos
2 CONC t unbearable.  Auntie seemed quite happy and unperturbed since there were no more bombs.  I had the first news from Austria before Xmas 1945 via the Red Cross and later established contact through an American girl stationed in Kar
2 CONC lsruhe who was sent on a mission to Vienna (we were not allowed to cross borders, least of all the Russian zone which separated the US zone from Vienna).  The news was tragic, our dad died in the last US air raid on Vienna on 12 M
2 CONC arch 1945, from a bomb shrapnel in his head.  The rest of our family survived but the fate of other relatives was unknown.  Prompted by the girl’s personal message from Auntie Gretel I started to send CARE food parcels to Vienna v
2 CONC ia the Red Cross.  In the spring of 1947 I was due for my second leave and realising that, like all good things, my service with the US army was coming to an end later that year (CCD was being phased out and some had already gone 
2 CONC home) I decided to take a holiday with mum at Berchtesgaden.  We had a wonderful time there, followed by a few outings in the Black Forest and bathing trips to the “Oelbecken” before I had to return to London in the summer of 1947
2 CONC .  It was a really sobering experience.  Problems seemed to be piling up right away.  I couldn’t marry mum in Karlsruhe because I would have exiled myself as a stateless refugee without a passport (but still with the old name).  S
2 CONC o I had to make an application to the Home Office and she had to make one to the Military Government in Karlsruhe for “denazification” clearance, a routine procedure every German who wanted to emigrate had to submit to.  It was bo
2 CONC und to take a long time even if I been a British serviceman.  My case as a stateless alien seemed hopeless.  There was also the unsolved problem of the name – we had agreed this gets top priority before we married (unthinkable to 
2 CONC inflict it on mum and possible offspring).
2 CONT
2 CONT I couldn’t get a job on returning to London and didn’t really want one yet, with £500 saved (I should think in present day terms about £15,000 - £20,000) I could afford to give myself time to find my feet again.  First of all I ha
2 CONC d to go to Dublin to collect my belongings which I couldn’t bring with me four years earlier.  Then Auntie Margaret was due on a visit to London.  She was working for the US Military Government in Vienna and they helped us getting
2 CONC  all the necessary papers for such a trip, which was impossible for an ordinary Austrian (even if absolutely blameless).  I was living in the back basement of 81 Hereford Road (Auntie still on top) and its condition and furniture 
2 CONC were appalling (armchair stuffed with old newspapers and wood shavings, store room was under the ancient, high iron bed, the whole house was gas lit, the stair case leading down to the back of the basement was pitch dark, even in 
2 CONC day time. Rent was 10/- = 50 pence per week, equivalent to £15 - £20 per week today).  Auntie Gretel got a shock when she saw our conditions (she was already in Strudelhofgasse).  In no time at all she fell out with Auntie Dorothy
2 CONC  over her stupid remarks about the Nazis (they were no threat as far as she could make out, what was all the fuss about?).  There was no mention of Fritz, I only learned of his existence much later through letters from Auntie Mart
2 CONC ha.  Auntie Lisi seemed in a bad way, homeless and having lost her husband Henry (died of TB I think), unable to look after Lotte who was with foster parents in Switzerland and went to school there.  I didn’t tell Auntie Gretel th
2 CONC at I was engaged, I had misgivings that we may be waiting in vain and never see each other again, in spite of regular correspondence.  I had known a number of Jewish girls and women I worked with in Germany.  Some of them were cle
2 CONC ver, sophisticated with respectable, even wealthy, backgrounds.  But none of them were attractive and compatible enough for me to overcome my prejudices and fears of perpetuating the curse that had haunted me almost from birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Auntie Gretel left I registered for work at the Edgware Road labour exchange and the ETU employment office in Grays Inn Road.  I was not entitled to any unemployment benefit (the only one in existence in those days) because 
2 CONC I lacked a stamped card for the last two years (I was prepared for that, that’s where my savings came in).  I actually never drew any unemployment benefit in periods of unemployment, some of them self inflicted by extended holiday
2 CONC s, in order to be able to decline the nastiest jobs and the dregs I usually was offered.  Some types of job I wasn’t allowed to take as an alien, e.g. telephone, BBC, Supply Industry (for which I had the final City and Guilds cert
2 CONC ificate in “Generation and Transmission”).  You had to be British born to be eligible for these jobs which reminded me of our second class citizen status under Hitler.  You could argue about the moral aspects of each case, but the
2 CONC re was no practical difference for the individual involved in either.
2 CONT
2 CONT The building trade and jobbing seemed to be the only kind of work accessible to me.  I was woefully short of experience in the former (had to learn about the construction of houses, i.e. walls, floors, ceilings, lofts, carcass wir
2 CONC ing, trunking etc.).  Large building sites were no problem, there were so many disputes, breaks and hold ups that you were only working half the time, anyway.  One man jobs where you were on your own (maybe with a useless mate) we
2 CONC re a different proposition altogether.
2 CONT
2 CONT From autumn 1947 to August 1948 I had 5 jobs.  On balance I didn’t earn enough and had to fall back on my savings.  It was a year full of distractions in which I couldn’t concentrate on anything.  There was some good news: The pol
2 CONC ice allowed us to register under a chosen name (for everyday use, but not for legal purposes).  Mum had got clearance and the Home Office seemed favourably disposed to our application.  The bad news: It was impossible to find a de
2 CONC cent flat I could afford.  Wages were about £5 to £6 per week gross (I had to allow for the possibility that I might get only 40 weeks’ work out of 52).  In the meantime mum had left home because of her new mother-in-law [step mot
2 CONC her?], Frau Speck and moved in with friends in Wuerzburg.  In the summer of 1948 I was offered a job as a holiday stand-in with Waterloo the Printers, of Park Royal.  It was for 3 months only to allow all the shift electricians to
2 CONC  take their holidays in turn.  The wages were excellent at £10 per week (double of the going rate outside even without extra shift allowance) for day work only – just the boost I needed for getting married.  When my 3 months stint
2 CONC  ended I was offered a job as maintenance and standby electrician at the same rate, with a staff option if a shift became available.  I told them I needed 1-2 weeks because I was getting married but they could not concede this bec
2 CONC ause my service was too short for any leave entitlement.  I needed time to go to the Home Office, the Bank of England (mum couldn’t buy a ticket with Marks, so I had to get permission to use sterling for this purpose) and to Cooks
2 CONC  to make the actual travel arrangements for her. (Every thing was very complicated in the years after the war because of currency restrictions and endless visas and border checks).  Most important: I also had to see a solicitor to
2 CONC  legalise the change of name by Deed Poll. [Here I have to digress: My old colleague Fuerst, a Jew from Vienna, whom I knew from the ETU “Studio 1” Branch in Shepherds Bush, came to my aid and supplied the solicitor, his friend wh
2 CONC o specialised in these cases.  He did it for £7 (about £250 now, I should think), very cheap even in those days.  Furst (he dropped the umlaut) worked in a film studio as an “electrician”.  This is actually a misnomer because they
2 CONC  are not skilled craftsmen but only shift, work and maintain the spot lights. All studio workers were extremely well paid with all sorts of extras.  Furst would easily have earned £10 to £15 per week.  It was 1948 and he was alrea
2 CONC dy buying a house in Burnt Oak!  How he got the job I never discovered – for a mere mortal it was like trying to get into heaven.  Talking about the ability of the Jews to make money while others struggle, I have to quote another 
2 CONC case, that of Mr Karrach whom I met in Dublin.  He got a job as the head porter at Grosvenor House in Park Lane and already had a substantial house in Cricklewood in 1947.  His sort of job required a big bribe I was told].  Mum ar
2 CONC rived on 4 September 1948 at Liverpool St. and I took her home by taxi.  I had tried my best to prepare her for the shock of the basement room and it seemed I had succeeded because she took it in her stride.  We got married on 25 
2 CONC September 1948 at Paddington Register Office and had our “reception” in the landlady’s front room.  She was a born German and took an instant liking to mum.  As a concession to our married life she allowed us to use the “scullery”
2 CONC  (a bare room opening into the back garden) as a kitchen, so I had a gas point installed there and bought a cooker and rigged up a few shelves which made mum quite happy.  In due course we chucked out the old bed and furniture and
2 CONC  bought a 3 piece suite with convertible sofa (put-u-up).  This was the beginning of married life – I still had to find a job.  The wedding didn’t cost much (maybe the equivalent of £300), and my recent earnings saved further inro
2 CONC ads on diminishing funds for the time being.  Problems were soon piling up, just as anticipated.  There was a massive housing shortage, a lot of run down slums with Paddington one of the worst boroughs and no council estates.  The
2 CONC  block opposite between Queensway and Inverness Terrace opposite Whiteleys, up to the post office was also an empty space.  So was the block from Porchester Road to Westbourne Terrace.  Brunel estate didn’t exist, it was all railw
2 CONC ay sidings and coal yards with mountains of coal from Wales dumped there.  The first council estate was built in the east end to honour Attlee.  It was a sensation and everybody went there (including us) to gaze at it as one of th
2 CONC e modern wonders.  There were also thousands of “prefabs” (prefabricated box like bungalows) on various cleared bomb sites (I used to service some on the North Circular Road when I worked for Willesden borough council).  There was
2 CONC  no chance for the likes of us, the blunt message everywhere was: “Our people come first”.  That was fully understood and accepted on my part.  There were no blacks or Asians in those days.  There would have been riots at the mere
2 CONC st hint of what is going on now.  There were relatively few foreigners, just the flotsam and jetsam of war (mainly Poles who couldn’t go back, among them, as it turned out, quite a few war criminals.  Their crimes, as yet unknown,
2 CONC  wouldn’t have registered with the ordinary British working class.  You’d meet people at work who’d tell you they should have let Hitler finish his job (exterminating Jews).  There was open discrimination everywhere (seemingly no 
2 CONC law against it).  Job ads and vacancy boards would specify: no foreigners, no Irish etc.  As always in times of shortages, there were Jews who waxed rich on it.  One of the most notorious of them was Rachmann, who bought thousands
2 CONC  of slum properties and hired them out at exorbitant rents and used violent methods to make tardy tenants pay up.  I have to mention this to illustrate the enormous difficulties and hopelessness of those times.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 7-8 months after mum’s arrival I had 4 jobs (mostly jobbing, with Irish firms, something that nobody wanted.  As I said in hard times I always got the dregs, left over at the labour exchange.  In the spring of 1949 I landed
2 CONC  a permanent job with the then Willesden (now Brent) borough council.  Pay was somewhat short of £7 per week for an initial 48 hour week (to illustrate money values then prevailing: a newspaper cost a penny, my second hand bike £1
2 CONC  and an early morning bus return to Stowbridge 4 pence.  Mum’s first job  was as a part time domestic in a posh flat in one of the big blocks near Marble Arch – Jews, of course.  This didn’t last long (I didn’t blame her) and the 
2 CONC labour exchange sent her to “Heinz” food factory in Harlesden as a “labeller”!  She made the best of a monotonous job and being German, took every thing she did very seriously and thereby outstripped all the other women on her pro
2 CONC duction line.  As a result she was hated and abused by them and eventually couldn’t stand it any more and left the job.  The money I was earning at Willesden Borough Council (with occasional over time) kept us going without resort
2 CONC ing to my savings, but mum wasn’t happy with our lot and I had to remind her that her people in Karlsruhe had even less.  She became homesick.
