2 NOTE Thank's Wayne!  I found the same certificate and just added it; I also made you manager just in case you have family photos to add at some point. 
2 NOTE Thanks, Dan and Ingrid.
3 CONT
3 CONT All of these bits of information were part of a trail that led to an image and translation of the marriage certificate from Landesarchiv (Provincial Archive) Berlin, accessible via ancestry.com.  Married 29 September 1902 in Berli
3 CONC n.  Certificate #453.  I'd attach the image if I could
2 NOTE even in those old days Vogel (which means bird) Victorine were old-fashioned and I think she was a bit ashamed and called herself Tony. Many Jewish women called themselves with traditional "modern" German names when beeing society
3 CONC  ladies.
3 CONT And Lotte short for Charlotte is quite normal even nowadays
2 NOTE Uri Shani screen-captured it from some German newspaper. It doesn't say on MyHeritage....  https://www.myheritage.com/photo-1732788_224678941_224678941/1?itemSource=individual&itemId=1705005
3 CONT
3 CONT Toni can be short for Victoria/Victorine, I guess.... :-) 
2 NOTE Please disregard my last post.  I see that you obtained the clip from a family tree.  I ultimately am looking for the original source of the clipping
2 NOTE What is the source of this news clip?
3 CONT
3 CONT Also, how was it determined that Vogel Victorine Eichenberg (nee, Wertheim) also went by the name Tony?
3 CONT
3 CONT Thank you
