1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-224678941-1-718646/moritz-gottschalk-in-myheritage-family-trees?indId=externalindividual-ca53f8e5a44fb38329b4d9724ac399d7&mrid=5c6510dd6fae3a002b81c57574e3
2 CONC 0568 MyHeritage Family Trees] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 29 2020, 1:42:37 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.hna.de/kassel/heute-steht-jahren-schloss-tuchfabrik-gottschalk-3831828.html Family Fabric History in German]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1999 the Kassel tent and canvas factory Gottschalk was forced to close after nearly 120 years in business. The descendants of co-founder Moritz Gottschalk have now handed over his estate to the city museum. We took a look at th
2 CONC e photographs and documents.
2 CONT Whether in war, in distant Africa, in the circus, or on vacation – Gottschalk’s tents were used worldwide for a wide variety of purposes for almost 120 years. The success story began in 1881, when Moritz Gottschalk (1851–1943) and
2 CONC  Johannes Coenning founded the company. Both had previously learned the trade under the Kassel real estate and textile entrepreneur Sigmund Aschrott, a pioneer of industrial textile production.
2 CONT After the construction of the tent and canvas factory on what is now Gottschalkstraße, difficult early years followed. Competition was fierce. In Kassel alone there were numerous competitors, including Salzmann, Fröhlich & Wolff, 
2 CONC Baumann & Lederer, and the Cassel Jute Spinning Mill. According to research by the city museum, Kassel’s textile industry was the city’s second-largest employer in the 1920s.
2 CONT Gottschalk also expanded, repeatedly enlarging its weaving and sewing halls. The company gained nationwide attention in 1895 when it produced the festival tent for the inauguration of the Kiel Canal (Nord-Ostsee Canal). However, i
2 CONC t did not only manufacture tents, but also ship sailcloth, awning fabrics, and carriage covers.
2 CONT In 1905, the Dieterici & Lebon weaving mill in Eschenstruth (Helsa) was taken over. With the outbreak of the First World War, Gottschalk benefited from orders for army tents. But with the global economic crisis, the company ran in
2 CONC to difficulties. Many of the 660 employees at the time were laid off.
2 CONT When the National Socialists came to power, the Jewish founder Moritz Gottschalk initially managed to continue directing the company’s affairs. To do so, he brought two National Socialists into the management in 1933. As the proce
2 CONC ss of Aryanization progressed, Jewish-owned weaving mills were boycotted. According to information from the city museum, they no longer received orders from the Reichswehr, the postal service, or the railways, and were allocated f
2 CONC ewer raw materials. Concentration camp imprisonment was also threatened. As a result, Gottschalk too was forced to leave the company in 1938, and it was attached to the Henschel works.
2 CONT After the end of the war, Gottschalk’s granddaughter Leni Frenzel (1915–1987) returned from exile and rebuilt the factory, which had been damaged during the war. Henschel was obliged to return the assets that had been confiscated 
2 CONC through Aryanization.
2 CONT From the 1950s onward, the company prospered again. Branch plants were established in Bettenhausen, Zierenberg, and Gensungen. By this time, Gottschalk was even producing sportswear, and in 1964 it outfitted the German team for th
2 CONC e Winter Olympic Games. In 1990, the Fulda-based Mehler AG took over the operation. However, revenues continued to decline. Textile production had become too expensive in Germany. In 1999, operations were shut down.
